@page "/learn/cacheview"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Caching part of a page · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Caching part of a page</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">One panel on a page runs a slow report query on every view. How do you cache just that panel?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Rendering · Static SSR · about 6 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> the <code>&lt;CacheView&gt;</code> component, which keeps the rendered HTML of
        part of a page. .NET 10 could already cache data with <code>IMemoryCache</code>, and whole responses with
        output caching. What it couldn't do was cache the rendered HTML of one part of a page, so the component
        behind it isn't even created on a hit.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a <i>Stock by category</i> page. Its report takes 1.5 seconds to calculate, so it caches itself for
        10 seconds with <code>IMemoryCache</code>, the usual .NET 10 answer. The page has two stamps:
        <b>Page rendered at</b> for the page, and <b>Figures calculated at</b> for the report.
    </p>
    <p>
        Under it, a line counts visitors and report queries since the app started, so the totals don't start at zero.
        The <b>Five visitors at once</b> button sends five requests at the same moment, like a busy morning, then
        reloads and adds a <b>Last burst</b> line beside the button saying how many queries those five ran. At rest,
        this demo and the .NET 11 one further down look the same; the difference only shows in a burst.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The report caches its own data" Url="demo/cacheview/stock/net10"
                Label=".NET 10: IMemoryCache in the component" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            Press <b>⟳</b> a few times. It comes back at once: <b>Page rendered at</b> moves and <b>Figures
            calculated at</b> stays, so hits work. Now wait until <b>Figures calculated at</b> is more than 10 seconds
            old, so the entry has expired, and press <b>Five visitors at once</b>. After the reload, the line beside
            the button reads <b>Last burst: 5 visitors, 5 queries ran</b>: every visitor ran the 1.5-second query.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>Every visitor who misses runs the query</h2>
    <p>Caching the data works while the entry is there. The trouble starts the moment it expires:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Five visitors arrive just after the entry expired</span>
        <pre class="snip">visitor 1  GetOrCreateAsync("stock-report")  <span class="err">miss → runs the query</span>
visitor 2  GetOrCreateAsync("stock-report")  <span class="err">miss → runs the query</span>
visitor 3  GetOrCreateAsync("stock-report")  <span class="err">miss → runs the query</span>
visitor 4  GetOrCreateAsync("stock-report")  <span class="err">miss → runs the query</span>
visitor 5  GetOrCreateAsync("stock-report")  <span class="err">miss → runs the query</span>
<span class="dim">5 × 1.5 s of database work, for one report</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        <code>IMemoryCache.GetOrCreateAsync</code> doesn't lock. Each visitor who finds no entry runs the factory, and
        nothing makes the others wait for the first. And the caching code lives inside the component: its key, its
        expiry, and the <code>@@inject</code>. Even on a hit, the component is still created and renders its table
        again. That's cheap for one table, but it's work on every request that caching the HTML would skip.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Wrap the slow part in a CacheView</h2>
    <p>Take the caching code out of the report, and wrap it where the page uses it.</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="hl">&lt;CacheView ExpiresAfter="TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10)"&gt;</span>
    &lt;StockSummary /&gt;
<span class="hl">&lt;/CacheView&gt;</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        On a miss, Blazor renders <code>StockSummary</code> as usual and keeps the HTML it produced. Visitors who miss
        at the same moment wait for that one render, and get its HTML. On a hit, it replays the HTML, and
        <code>StockSummary</code> isn't created at all. The rest of the page, outside the CacheView, still renders on
        every request. There's nothing to set up in <code>Program.cs</code>: unlike <code>IMemoryCache</code>, which
        needs <code>AddMemoryCache()</code>, CacheView keeps its entries in memory by default. Here's the same page,
        with the same report, wrapped.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same page, with CacheView" Url="demo/cacheview/stock"
                Label=".NET 11: CacheView">
        <Caption>
            Press <b>⟳</b> a few times: from outside, hits look the same as in Fig. 1. Then wait until <b>Figures
            calculated at</b> is more than 10 seconds old, and press <b>Five visitors at once</b>. After the reload,
            the line beside the button reads <b>Last burst: 5 visitors, 1 query ran</b>: the other four waited for the
            first visitor's render.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>
        Two report components are involved. <code>StockSummaryNet10</code> is the .NET 10 one, with its own caching
        code; <code>StockSummary</code> is the same component with that code taken out. Both hand their rows to a
        shared <code>StockTable</code>, which draws the table and the <b>Figures calculated at</b> stamp. Here's the
        .NET 10 report component, then the .NET 11 page that wraps the plain one:
    </p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/Caching/StockSummaryNet10.razor" Feature="cacheview">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 1's report component. Fig. 1's page just renders <code>&lt;StockSummaryNet10 /&gt;</code>. The
            component picks its own key, <code>"stock-report"</code>, and its own expiry, and every visitor who misses
            runs the factory.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/Caching/StockReport.razor" Feature="cacheview">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's page, <code>StockReport.razor</code>. The change is the CacheView around
            <code>StockSummary</code>, which is Fig. 3's component with the caching code removed: it runs the query in
            <code>OnInitializedAsync</code> and nothing else.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Cache the data, or cache the whole page</h2>
    <p>Blazor in .NET 10 had nothing for caching the HTML of part of a page. We built both usual workarounds on .NET 10:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Two ways to cache the report in .NET 10, and what a .NET 10 build did</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">@@inject IMemoryCache Cache
<span class="err">Cache.GetOrCreateAsync("stock-report", ...)</span></pre>
                <span class="why">fast on hits, but needs <code>AddMemoryCache()</code>, and the component is still created on every request</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ five visitors who miss together run the query five times</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">@@page "/stock"
<span class="err">@@attribute [OutputCache(Duration = 10)]</span></pre>
                <span class="why">fast, but all or nothing: the page's own stamp freezes too</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ add a form, and its antiforgery token sends <code>Set-Cookie</code> and <code>no-store</code>: nothing is cached, and every view takes 1.5 s, with no warning</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        The stampede on its own did have a .NET 10 answer: <code>HybridCache</code> (.NET 9 and later, in the
        <code>Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.Hybrid</code> package). Its <code>GetOrCreateAsync</code> makes concurrent
        misses for the same key wait for one factory call. But it still caches data, so the component is still
        created and rendered on every request, and the key and expiry still live in the component.
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">CacheView in a .NET 10 project</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;CacheView ExpiresAfter="TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10)"&gt;</pre>
        <div class="verdict bad">✗ not a build error: only warning RZ10012, "unexpected name 'CacheView'". The page sends a literal <code>&lt;CacheView&gt;</code> tag to the browser and stays slow on every request.</div>
    </div>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>Cache the HTML, not the data</h2>
    <p>
        What .NET 11 adds is the layer above the data: the rendered output. The component inside a CacheView is
        skipped entirely on a hit, which no data cache can do, and the caching decision moves out of the component
        to the page that uses it.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>Every visitor gets the same cached HTML.</b> The key is where the CacheView sits, plus <code>CacheKey</code> if you set one. The user, query string and route aren't in it, so a panel that shows one user's data will be served to the next user too. Add <code>VaryByUser</code>, <code>VaryByQuery</code>, <code>VaryByRoute</code> or <code>VaryByCulture</code> for anything that differs per visitor.</li>
        <li><b>Entries last 30 seconds unless you say otherwise.</b> Set <code>ExpiresAfter</code> or <code>ExpiresOn</code> to change that. <code>ExpiresSliding</code> alone doesn't lift the 30-second limit, so pair it with one of the other two.</li>
        <li><b>Keep one small part fresh inside a cached one.</b> Mark a component <code>[CacheBehavior(CacheBehavior.Rerender)]</code> and it runs on every request, even on a hit. The app's dashboard uses one to say how old its cached figures are.</li>
        <li><b>Only static server-rendered GETs are cached.</b> Form posts render as usual, and so do CacheViews inside a <code>[StreamRendering]</code> component. Use it on the static parts of a page, not inside an interactive island.</li>
        <li><b>Already using <code>HybridCache</code>?</b> When one is registered, CacheView stores its entries there instead of its own in-memory store.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
